# frozen_string_literal: true

# McpController is the app-owned HTTP entry point in front of the MCP transport.
# It inherits ApplicationController so it reuses YOUR app's infrastructure
# (request/response, logging, middleware). It is a MACHINE endpoint, not a
# browser one: the inherited browser-oriented filters below (CSRF, an auth
# before_action that 302-redirects to a login page) are NOT appropriate here and
# are turned off / must be skipped — do not assume ApplicationController's
# before_action stack works unchanged for this token endpoint.
#
# This file is APP-OWNED and yours to edit. The rails_mcp gem ships no
# authentication and no policy (ADR-0004): it defines *where* authentication
# goes; you define *what* it is.
#
# SECURITY: as stamped, the `/mcp` endpoint is UNAUTHENTICATED UNTIL you secure
# it here. It is FAIL-CLOSED by default — the authentication seam below raises,
# so every request is denied and no tool runs until you wire your real check.
# That is intentional: a misconfigured install denies rather than exposes.
class McpController < ApplicationController
  # CSRF is a browser defense (it validates a token stored in the session). An MCP
  # client is a machine sending a cookieless JSON POST with no CSRF token; if your
  # ApplicationController has `protect_from_forgery with: :exception`, every request
  # here would raise InvalidAuthenticityToken. Turn it off for this machine endpoint
  # and rely on the authentication seam below instead.
  skip_forgery_protection

  # === SKIP INHERITED BROWSER AUTH FILTERS (edit for your app) ==============
  # If ApplicationController runs a browser-oriented auth before_action (e.g. Devise
  # `authenticate_user!`, or your own filter that 302-redirects to a login page),
  # it will REDIRECT a machine MCP client instead of letting it authenticate below.
  # Skip it here and let `authenticate_acting_user!` be the sole check for /mcp:
  #
  #   skip_before_action :authenticate_user!   # Devise, or your own browser auth filter
  #
  # Leave this commented until you know which inherited filters your app applies.

  # One action serves every MCP request verb (POST, GET, DELETE). It authenticates
  # the request, resolves the acting user your app resolves, then serves the request on the
  # official `mcp` gem's public per-request pattern. The transport — not this action —
  # decides per-verb behavior.
  def handle
    user = authenticate_acting_user!

    # Build a FRESH MCP::Server for THIS request, carrying `user` on server_context
    # so per-request identity is never mutated on a shared, process-wide server
    # (per-request isolation). `RegisteredTools.all` is the allow-list — the exact set
    # of tools this endpoint lists and can call, resolved fresh per request (reload-safe).
    # Both lines are yours to edit: swap in your own list to serve a different tool set
    # on this route.
    server = MCP::Server.new(
      name: "rails_mcp",
      tools: RegisteredTools.all,
      server_context: {user: user}
    )

    # The stateless streamable-HTTP transport is the `mcp` gem's Rack transport.
    # `allowed_hosts:` widens the SDK's DNS-rebinding guard beyond loopback to YOUR
    # app's host allow-list, so a real production Host (e.g. "boswell.fly.dev", set in
    # config.hosts) is not rejected with `403 Forbidden: Invalid Host header`. We pass
    # only the String entries of config.hosts (it may also hold Regexp/IPAddr, which the
    # guard cannot compare).
    #
    # WARNING — the DNS-rebinding guard is OFF until config.hosts is populated.
    # `config.hosts` is auto-populated only in DEVELOPMENT; a standard PRODUCTION app
    # leaves it EMPTY. If we hard-enabled the guard, an empty config.hosts would fall
    # back to loopback-only and 403 EVERY real request ("Invalid Host header") on day one.
    # So we tie `dns_rebinding_protection:` to whether config.hosts actually holds String
    # hosts: the guard is ON once you populate config.hosts, and OFF (fails open on the
    # Host/Origin check only) while it is empty. Authentication and authorization remain
    # fail-closed regardless. TO RE-ENABLE THE GUARD: add your production host to
    # config.hosts (e.g. `config.hosts << "your-app.example.com"`), OR validate the Host
    # header at your proxy, OR force it on here with `dns_rebinding_protection: true`
    # (which reinstates the loopback-only 403 until config.hosts is populated).
    #
    # Add `allowed_origins:` here for your deploy. `handle_request` takes this
    # controller's request and returns a Rack response triple.
    transport = MCP::Server::Transports::StreamableHTTPTransport.new(
      server,
      stateless: true,
      allowed_hosts: Rails.application.config.hosts.grep(String),
      dns_rebinding_protection: Rails.application.config.hosts.grep(String).any?
    )

    # === OPTIONAL: REQUEST-SCOPED CONTEXT (edit for your app) ================
    # The gem's invoke pipeline runs `authorize` before `perform` within this same
    # `handle_request` call. If your app needs a request-scoped context that BOTH
    # `authorize` and `perform` see, wrap `handle_request` here to scope the whole
    # call — scoping inside a single tool's `perform` is too late, because
    # `authorize` has already run. Resolve the context from the acting user or the
    # request, then wrap it:
    #
    #   status, headers, body = SomeContext.wrap do
    #     transport.handle_request(request)
    #   end
    #
    # The unscoped default below is correct when no such context is needed.
    status, headers, body = transport.handle_request(request)

    # Render the Rack triple back through the controller. Customize rendering here.
    headers.each { |key, value| response.headers[key] = value }
    self.response_body = body
    self.status = status
  end

  private

  # === AUTHENTICATION SEAM (required) =====================================
  # Resolve the acting user your app resolves for THIS request from the app's own
  # auth stack, then return it. It is the only identity handed to the gem; the gem
  # never invents one.
  #
  # As stamped this RAISES (fail closed) so the endpoint denies until you wire it.
  # Replace the raise with your real check, e.g. Devise / a session / a token:
  #
  #   def authenticate_acting_user!
  #     authenticate_user!            # Devise, or your own before-action auth
  #     current_user                  # the resolved acting user
  #   end
  #
  # or a bearer token. Store the token DIGESTED at rest (never the raw token), and
  # parse the header with an ANCHORED scheme regex. A global substring strip of the
  # scheme prefix neither requires the `Bearer ` scheme nor survives a token that itself
  # contains that substring — use the anchored regex below instead. Add an
  # `api_token_digest` column and look up by the SHA-256 digest; the raw token is shown
  # to the operator once and treated like a password (never logged):
  #
  #   def authenticate_acting_user!
  #     token = request.headers["Authorization"].to_s[/\ABearer (.+)\z/, 1]
  #     user = User.find_by(api_token_digest: Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(token)) if token
  #     raise RailsMcp::NotAuthorized, "no acting user" if user.nil?
  #     user
  #   end
  #
  # The digest lookup is the constant-time-safe form: the app stores a SHA-256 digest,
  # not the raw secret, so an equality match on the digest column never compares the raw
  # token. Do NOT store or compare a raw `api_token`.
  def authenticate_acting_user!
    raise RailsMcp::NotAuthorized,
      "McpController#authenticate_acting_user! is not implemented — the /mcp " \
      "endpoint is unauthenticated until secured, so every request is denied " \
      "until you wire your real authentication check here."
  end
end
